## TASK (template)
Design the whole feature in one pass and submit it to DES yourself.
- Read the authority documents first. They are the DISCUSS handoff. A decision it marks `Proposed` is unconfirmed: confirm or change it in your design and say which. An item it marks `Open` and assigns to design needs a decision in `decisions`, or a stated reason it stays open and who owns it.
- Order: the shared decisions first (one manifest, submitted with `--shared`), then one manifest per value as a delta on the shared design. Each value manifest still carries the inherited common constraints.
- The architecture brief DES writes from your manifests is the descriptive document of the whole architecture, for every later reader. Write `purpose`, the shared design and each value's delta so that a reader who knows nothing of this Request can see the parts of the architecture, what each part is responsible for, and the rules that bind them.
- The next role, the acceptance designer, does not read your reply. It reads that brief and a handoff DES derives for it from your manifests. Everything it needs to author the executable oracle of a value must be in your manifest for that value: the driving port, the public observations, the test substrate and the runner argv, the architectural and contract obligations, and the names of the typed carriers and invariants.

## ALWAYS REQUIRED (owner's rule; none of these waits for a selection)
Default in every design: algebraic modeling with formal verification. Type thinking, correctness by construction and property-based testing remain separately assessed obligations. Choose checkers by need: Quint for state models, TLA+/TLC when Quint is insufficient for the claim, Agda for proofs, and both/all when one checker is insufficient. State which checker covers each claim and why in `decisions`. Before you design, Read these installed skills with the Read tool (some cannot be loaded with the Skill tool) and say in `decisions` which ones you read:
- ~/.claude/skills/nw-solution-architect-formal-verification/SKILL.md
- ~/.claude/skills/nw-algebraic-design-protocol/SKILL.md
- ~/.claude/skills/nw-certainty-by-construction/SKILL.md
- ~/.claude/skills/nw-type-level-design/SKILL.md
- ~/.claude/skills/nw-property-based-testing/SKILL.md
Then put in the manifests, in `decisions`:
- formal verification of the state, concurrency, recovery and local proof claims of this design with the selected checker(s): the model/proof you wrote, its backend and bounds, each property, the literal argv and exit status of every checker run (`quint typecheck`, `quint run` labelled as simulation, `quint verify`, TLC with its model/configuration, or Agda for the proof), the tool version, the bounds or seed, the result, the raw trace of every counterexample, and for each property a mutant that violates it with the checker's counterexample/rejection as receipt. Put the complete text of each model/proof in `decisions` as an entry of its own, unedited and with every line break kept, together with its sha256 and the path where it is to be committed. A model/proof that exists only in a scratch directory, or only as a hash, is not delivered. For every step of the model name the code mechanism that makes it atomic, or the seam where it is cut, as file and symbol; a step with no such mechanism is model-only, and you say so. Probe each needed tool before relying on it: retain exact argv and output (`quint --version`, `java -version`, `agda --version`, and the resolved TLC backend/jar as applicable). No tool installation is assumed. A claim whose checker did not run is INDETERMINATE, never PASS on prose.
- liveness as its own claim when relevant; state a concrete non-applicability reason otherwise. Choose TLA+/TLC when Quint is insufficient for the temporal/fairness claim; experimental Apalache temporal support and an interactive question are not a completed check. For Quint/TLC, run `quint verify <file>.qnt --init=init --step=step --temporal=<property> --backend tlc` after observing that backend's availability; for direct TLA+, retain and pass the `.tla` model and `.cfg` configuration to the probed TLC jar. TLC needs a finite state graph, so check liveness on a slice of the model that keeps only the variables the property reads: drop ghost histories and unbounded counters only while preserving the temporal behavior, and name the slice's limits. Write the fairness you assume as a temporal formula (`weakFair(action, vars)` or `strongFair`) and state it in `decisions`. Record three runs with argv and exit status: the property without fairness (expected: a counterexample that ends in stuttering, the do-nothing behavior; keep the trace, or explain why the actual property already holds), the property under your fairness (expected: pass), and a mutant that removes the progress or release step (expected: a counterexample under the same fairness). Put the complete text of the slice in `decisions` as an entry of its own, like the model. Liveness is PASS only when these runs happened; otherwise its DESIGN axis is BLOCKED and you say why.
- missing needed checker: stop that proof and return a typed refusal through the caller's enforced schema, naming the missing tools, affected claims and platform-specific install commands. Offer the human two choices through the host: install the tools, or explicitly waive that verification. As a subagent you do not ask the human yourself or install without authorization. Do not silently downgrade, claim PASS or fake a run. Only a recorded human waiver permits continuation without that proof; record its affected claims and reason in `decisions`. The waived claim remains visibly INDETERMINATE with the waiver reason, never PASS. A refusal to install is not a waiver.
- type thinking: the invalid states your types make unrepresentable and the ones they cannot; state each type-safety claim at the strength of the checker this repository actually runs. State each exclusion in one sentence: the call or state excluded, who could still construct it, the harm, and the admitted input, constructor, consumer and public observation. For each observation of your design state what it cannot distinguish. When a representation changes, claim both round trips, and for a mapping over an operation claim that identity and composition are kept; run the laws over generated inputs against a reference encoding, including the input that refuted the old representation, and keep the receipt.
- property-based testing: the properties to check with generated inputs through the public surface, each generator domain with its size bounds, the shrinking approach, and an independent observation or falsifier for each broad domain. If the repository has no property-testing library, design seeded generators and shrinking by hand. Reuse the invariants of the selected formal model as properties.
- an architectural check marked applicable whenever the authority evidences a deliberate dependency, boundary or owner law that the change can violate.
- code facts: before you claim reuse, impact, a caller or a reader of a symbol, or that a path is never wired, resolve the fact with `des code-fact` (the same `des` command you submit with; Read ~/.claude/skills/nw-code-analysis-port/SKILL.md first; queries: `query.callers-of`, `query.reads-of`, `query.atoms-in-file`, `query.never-wired`, `query.adr-section`). Name each query, its subject, and the provider and confidence it printed, in `decisions`. If the command degrades or fails, say so there; use Glob and Grep only as the fallback and say which facts came from them.

## BUDGET (quality is not reduced; waste is)
- Run checkers one at a time. Apalache uses a fixed port, so parallel `quint verify` runs fail. Time budget for one `quint verify`: 180 s; prefix it with `timeout 180`; the same budget holds for each TLC run. Start from the smallest instance that still exercises the property (two processes, the fewest steps that reach the seam under test) and enlarge it only while a run finishes inside the budget, in this order: two processes, three processes, failures added, liveness added; record where each stage stopped. A run that exceeds the budget is not repeated at the same bound: record the bound it reached and mark the claim INDETERMINATE. To kill a mutant, run `quint run --invariant` on it first (a counterexample it finds is a valid kill with the same receipt rules); run `quint verify` on a mutant only when `quint run` finds none.
- You have 40 turns in total and the manifests must be submitted inside them. Batching work saves turns; it never replaces the required probes of the host tools or the reading of the skills above. Submit the shared manifest by the time you have used about 25 turns, then the values. A run that ends without a submission delivers nothing.

