## IN ADDITION, PUT IN THE MANIFEST (template)
- the cross-layer algebra of your Required Design Pass (item 6), in `decisions`: one entry per affected layer (domain, application/port, adapter/integration, infrastructure/recovery) with its states, operations, observations and laws; state each law as a claim a test could check
- constructive closure (nw-design, Constructive closure and refinement), in `decisions`: safety, inhabitation, public reachability and liveness, each with its DESIGN axis (exactly PASS or BLOCKED; a claim that was not checked is BLOCKED) and its runtime axis (exactly OBSERVED, DESIGNED_NOT_BUILT or INDETERMINATE)
- residuality (Required Design Pass item 7), in `decisions`: derive each stressor by negating one declared assumption of your design, and name that assumption; give one row per stressor with the observation it must preserve, the degradation it may allow, the outcome it forbids and the residue that survives it; name the single points of failure and the bypasses the design cannot stop (deserialization, reflection, casts, writers outside your code, foreign processes, process failure), each with the remaining obligation and its owner, or the reason it is unreachable; walk a second set of stressors, not used to derive the design, against the finished design and record what each showed; and give the admitted, excluded and unknown states with their risk owner
- self-falsification (nw-design), in `decisions`: the concrete counterexamples you tried against your own design and what each showed; when one refutes a law, say whether the cause is a missing carrier distinction, a law scope problem or a boundary problem
- every bound, range or absence of a bound that the cited authority states for inputs you store or validate, in `constraints`; name as a deviation any place where your design is narrower or wider than the authority
- each bounded probe you ran, in `decisions`: the unknown it addressed, the fact and the result
- the typed carriers of your design, in `decisions`: for decisions, effects, errors and per-process state, the type or record, and the module that owns it
- your design assessment with Jev (nw-typesafe-system-one), in `decisions`: the typed questions you asked about your design decisions, boundaries and producer/consumer agreements, asked at each decision point where you chose between alternatives or judged a reuse candidate, and for each the answer with its confidence, your check against the original evidence, and the action you took; the model name Jev reported, and tokens, duration and cost when it prints them (`not available` when it does not); if Jev could not be used, `typesafe: unavailable` and why

## SUBMIT (native host-owned contract; overrides source self-submission wording above)
For each selected shared or value invocation, return one closed envelope with `outcome`, opaque `diagnostic`, and the requested complete schema_version 1 `manifest` on acceptance. The host extracts only that accepted manifest and submits it unchanged on stdin:
{{submit_steps|bullets}}
Do not execute these constructor commands or edit authority, ADR, handover, production or oracle files; DES is the sole authority writer. Return only the requested closed envelope, retaining every measured obligation above in an accepted manifest. Rejected or indeterminate envelopes omit `manifest` or set it to `null` and give a nonempty diagnostic naming the missing facts or finding and its remediation. A finding names `DEFECT-OWNER: <owner>`. The host alone submits accepted manifests and owns human interaction, further selected submissions and any constructor retry. Never return legacy provider facts, bare manifests, side-channel manifest files or DELIVERY-OUTCOME/NEXT summaries in place of the envelope; those summaries are DES observations after host submission, not your return contract.
If you cannot execute a DESIGN-required verification or lack an authorization or input, return `rejected` through the caller's enforced schema with `BLOCKED-BY: <owner>` in `diagnostic`, naming the precise prerequisite (for example `formal checks not authorized` or `checker unavailable at <path>`). Use a typed blocker field only if that schema provides it; leave the refused design payload null, never put a candidate manifest in `diagnostic`, and never return `accepted`.

